# Trivy ignore file — scoped exceptions only, each with its reason written here.
#
# This file exists so that known, documented, upstream-unfixed findings do not either hide behind
# a blanket `ignore-unfixed` or hold the whole pipeline red. Anything NOT listed here still
# fails the build at CRITICAL/HIGH, and `ignore-unfixed: false` is unchanged.
#
# Every entry below is a HIGH CVE in a Debian trixie base package for which Debian has released
# NO fix. The image is `python:3.12-slim` = trixie, the Dockerfile runs `apt-get upgrade`, and
# these eight survive it. Debian's security tracker marks each source package vulnerable in
# trixie and fixed only in forky/sid; Docker Hub publishes no forky variant of the `python`
# image, so there is no patched base to move to.
#
# This is a base-image problem, not an NYX problem: none of the vulnerable code paths are NYX's,
# and the container runs as a non-root user (uid 10001). The real remedy is a fixed base image.
# Re-check each of these when trixie gets a DSA or a forky-based Python image is published, and
# delete the entry then.

# --- util-linux (bsdutils, libblkid1, libmount1, libsmartcols1, liblastlog2-2, mount, login) ---
# Mount-helper and bind-mount handling. Each <no-dsa>, classified minor by Debian.
#   mount-helper exit-status check; X-mount.idmap / X-mount.owner escalation
CVE-2026-76642
#   nsenter --join-cgroup leaks root
CVE-2026-78408
#   X-mount.subdir detached-tree handling
CVE-2026-78409
#   restricted bind mounts do not pin the source
CVE-2026-78410

# --- systemd (libsystemd0, libudev1): vulnerable in trixie, fixed in forky/sid ---
CVE-2026-16742

# --- ncurses (libtinfo6, libncursesw6, ncurses-base): <no-dsa>, minor; fixed in forky/sid ---
CVE-2025-69720

# --- acl (libacl1): <no-dsa>; Debian will fix first in unstable; fixed only in forky/sid ---
CVE-2026-54369

# --- perl (perl-base): vulnerable in trixie, fixed in forky/sid ---
CVE-2026-9538
